  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...This afternoon, a warm front stretches west to \neast across northern NC. Based on surface obs, the eastern end \nof the front has recently cleared north of the Albemarle \nSound/NRN OBX area, putting all of ENC in a broad warm sector \nthat encompasses much of the Southeast U.S. \n\nSouth of the front, there appears to be some residual \nsubsidence/capping in the wake of the shortwave that moved \noffshore this morning, and for now this appears to be delaying \nthe onset of cumulus development. Continued southerly flow and \nmoisture advection plus large-scale forcing for ascent with the \narrival of the next shortwave (currently moving out of SC) \nshould prove sufficient for the development of deep convection \nin a zone along the inland- advancing seabreeze. With deeper \nsoutherly flow present, the seabreeze should be more progressive\ntoday, and the duration of higher rainfall rates should be \nlimited over any one area. Even so, PWATs of 2\"+ and moderate \ninstability should support areas of heavy rainfall along with \nthe potential for minor/nuisance-style flooding, especially in \nurban and poor drainage areas. The risk of flash flooding looks \nlow today, though, given the expected progressive nature of \nconvection today. Deep, moist profiles today will favor lowered \nDCAPE compared to yesterday. This plus weak deep layer shear \nshould keep the risk of severe weather LOW.\n\nThe environment on Sunday looks fairly similar to today's. \nHowever, deep layer shear is forecast to be ever-so-slightly \nhigher (25kt Sunday compared to 15kt today). This may allow a \nslightly improved storm organization potential and perhaps a \nslightly better chance of a few storms reaching severe, or near-\nsevere, levels. The primary risk should be strong winds, but \nperhaps some hail can occur in the tallest, most sustained, \ncores. The focus for convection should be the seabreeze and an \ninland trough. The low-level flow may veer to southwesterly, \nhelping keep the seabreeze less progressive compared to today, \nand storms may not be as progressive. With a high PWAT airmass \nstill in place, this could allow a slight bump up in the risk \nfor urban and poor drainage flooding, with at least a low-end \nrisk of flash flooding.\n\nFinally, a cold front will sweep south through the area on \nMonday. Guidance continues to trend on the slower side with the \npassage, and now this sets up the potential for the front to \nmove through during peak heating on Monday. This will be \nsomething to watch as deep layer shear is forecast to be higher \n(~30kt), and may support a strong/severe thunderstorm risk \nconditional on sufficient instability being realized. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Upper level ridging quickly rebuilds across the\nSouthern U.S. next week. Meanwhile, a notably strong surface \nhigh will nose south along the lee of the Appalachians. \nSynoptically this is a very dry pattern for the region. Despite \nwarming low-level thicknesses beneath the ridge, the \nnortheasterly flow around the high should help to hold temps \ndown some. Additionally, the airmass will be drier with less \nhumidity, making it feel not as hot. It will feel a little more \nlike fall, especially at night and in the morning.",
